00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:23.040
You've probably gone through something like this&nbsp;
before. You have a loaf of bread sitting around,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:28.880
waiting to be eaten. And you fully intend to eat&nbsp;
it, you might even have had the perfect sandwich&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:35.440
in mind. But then you go grab some slices and you&nbsp;
notice that someone…or something…got to it first.&nbsp;

00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:40.640
It’s the mold, the dreaded&nbsp;
hairy patches of black, blue,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:46.720
green, and gray expanding like a circular&nbsp;
lawn intent on taking over your bread.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:57.840
It seems to come out of nowhere, but once&nbsp;
it arrives, there is no getting rid of it.&nbsp;

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:07.040
If you’ve been following our journey through the&nbsp;
microcosmos for a while now, you might remember&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:13.520
that we talked about slime molds before. So maybe&nbsp;
it seems to you that we have covered mold already.&nbsp;

00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:18.560
Except that when it comes to nature, it&nbsp;
turns out that historically, humans have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:24.880
not been so great at naming things, which is not&nbsp;
entirely our fault. We have, for obvious reasons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:29.520
tended to use similar names to describe&nbsp;
organisms that look similar to each other.&nbsp;

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:35.840
And slime molds kind of resemble bread mold,&nbsp;
with the way they both fly around the world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:41.760
as small spores, and the way that they both&nbsp;
creep and spread across surfaces. But this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:49.040
resemblance hides some pretty big differences.
For example, a slime mold is a protozoan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:54.720
which means that the large organism you&nbsp;
see is actually a single-celled eukaryote&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.720 --> 00:02:00.000
able to push the boundaries of its one&nbsp;
cell further and further and further.&nbsp;

00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:04.720
Mold, regular old mold, like&nbsp;
the kind you see on bread,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:13.520
are not anything like that. They are an entirely&nbsp;
different kingdom, in fact. They... are fungi.&nbsp;

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:17.520
And there are a few fungal species that are&nbsp;
known to make up bread mold specifically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:23.040
the most common of which are the multicellular&nbsp;
Rhizopus, Penicillium, and Cladosporium2.&nbsp;

00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:30.240
As we said, fungi are their own kingdom of&nbsp;
life, just like protozoans and plants. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:35.280
while their growth and way of life might make&nbsp;
them sometimes resemble members of other kingdoms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:41.040
like plants and protozoans, it turns out fungi&nbsp;
are the animal’s kingdom’s closest relations3&nbsp;

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:50.320
Of course, family relations mean nothing&nbsp;
when there’s food on the table. And fungi&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:55.520
are not like us. They can’t just go to the&nbsp;
store to pick up a loaf of bread. They can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.520 --> 00:03:03.280
however travel, flying through air or swimming&nbsp;
through water as spores. These spores are kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:09.280
like seeds, waiting for the right combination of&nbsp;
temperature, acidity, and moisture to take root.&nbsp;

00:03:09.280 --> 00:03:14.160
And for some lucky spores, bread can&nbsp;
potentially provide those conditions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.720 --> 00:03:20.880
Especially pre-sliced bread, which has all&nbsp;
those nutritious nooks and crannies to grow in.&nbsp;

00:03:21.520 --> 00:03:27.760
And mold are very good at growing. Starting from&nbsp;
the spore, the fungus begins to expand in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:34.160
form of threads called hyphae. The hyphae grow at&nbsp;
the tips, their walls made out of the tough but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.160 --> 00:03:40.400
flexible chitin that allows them to get creative.&nbsp;
Sometimes the hyphae form branches of their own,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:47.520
and sometimes they will fuse with one another.
The result is a web of hyphae called the mycelium.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:52.400
But the mycelium is not just a matter&nbsp;
of spreading. It’s a network that acts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.400 --> 00:03:58.800
as a feeding apparatus. The hyphae will secrete&nbsp;
enzymes into the bread around it, breaking down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:06.160
its nutrients so that the fungus can eat. It’s&nbsp;
basically just a really large network of mouths.&nbsp;

00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:10.560
This makes for a frustrating sight&nbsp;
when we’re looking at our despondent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:16.400
gross slices of bread. But when you see a piece of&nbsp;
mold growing and eating away at a piece of bread,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.400 --> 00:04:27.840
you are seeing a loose approximation of the&nbsp;
same processes that made our world possible.&nbsp;

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:34.320
We’ve seen this before with lichen, a&nbsp;
collaboration between fungi and algae that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.320 --> 00:04:40.400
breaks down rocks into the mineral components&nbsp;
that sustain other forms of life. And fungi&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.400 --> 00:04:47.040
can break down so much more. There are&nbsp;
even species in Chernobyl, eating away&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:53.600
at radioactive material like hot graphite
Fungi, they could eat the world, if they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.600 --> 00:05:00.000
wanted to. But their path is much more subtle&nbsp;
than their fuzzy appearance might suggest.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:05.920
Because fungi don’t just tear things apart.&nbsp;
They also form relationships—significant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.920 --> 00:05:12.080
essential relationships—with other organisms.
A plant’s roots, for example, might become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.080 --> 00:05:18.080
intimately entwined with a fungi’s hyphae,&nbsp;
forming a bond we call the mycorrhiza.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.360 --> 00:05:25.360
And as fungi hook themselves up to different&nbsp;
plants, they can connect the plants themselves,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.360 --> 00:05:31.440
serving as living bridges to exchange everything&nbsp;
from nutrients to chemical warning messages.&nbsp;

00:05:32.160 --> 00:05:36.800
So it is tempting, when writing about fungi,&nbsp;
to treat them as something that needs to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.800 --> 00:05:42.640
redeemed. After all, for many of us, fungi&nbsp;
are the thing that rots. They put an end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.640 --> 00:05:48.480
to our bread, our fruits, and our snacks.
And It’s so easy to redeem them when they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.480 --> 00:05:54.800
clear the world for us. When the bread mold&nbsp;
that disgusts us also famously supplied us with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.800 --> 00:05:59.840
penicillin, an antibiotic that after&nbsp;
its accidental discovery in mold&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.840 --> 00:06:05.680
went on to save countless lives.
But fungi, however prevalent they may be,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.680 --> 00:06:12.480
are a challenge to pin down. In his book Entangled&nbsp;
Life, the mycologist Merlin Sheldrake writes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.040 --> 00:06:18.560
“I have tried to find ways to enjoy the&nbsp;
ambiguities that fungi present, but it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.560 --> 00:06:26.160
not always easy to be comfortable in the space&nbsp;
created by open questions. Agoraphobia can set in.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.160 --> 00:06:33.840
It’s tempting to hide in small rooms built from&nbsp;
quick answers. I have done my best to hold back.”&nbsp;

00:06:37.280 --> 00:06:42.080
Ambiguity is not difficult to find in&nbsp;
nature, especially when we’re venturing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.080 --> 00:06:48.160
into the microcosmos. These are organisms&nbsp;
that speak to a world that long pre-dates us,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.720 --> 00:06:54.960
and in their enduring nature, they suggest a&nbsp;
bit of what the world may look like after us.&nbsp;

